// Authentication-failure burst: pause when failed sign-ins against one monitored
// surface reach a ceiling inside a five-minute window - the measurable core of a
// credential-stuffing or brute-force control. AUTH_FAILURES_5M is the GLOBAL
// count of failed authentication attempts against that surface, across all
// accounts and sources, over the trailing five minutes, as the host counts them.
// REGIME: all of them. Abuse of a login surface is not tied to a market or a
// release; the rule is armed whenever the surface is exposed.
// CONDITIONS: none in the rule. The host collects the events, defines what a
// failure is, owns the window boundary, and performs any lockout or block. Nano
// reads no log, locks no account and blocks no address; it proposes a pause and
// the host's DecisionGate decides.
// INVALIDATION: the window draining below the ceiling. The rule does not latch;
// how long a lockout lasts is host policy.
// SHAPE: 1m over a 5m trailing count; frequent enough to catch a burst while it
// is running, and the window - not this cadence - absorbs single mistyped
// passwords, which is why there is no separate observe tier here.
// NOT credential_age_alert: that reviews a credential that is merely getting
// old. This one reacts to someone failing to use credentials right now.
// CALIBRATED ON: a placeholder policy figure, 50 global failures in five
// minutes for one surface. It does not travel: it scales with the surface's
// legitimate traffic, and a per-account count is a different control that
// needs a far lower ceiling - publish it under its own signal name.
strategy AuthFailureBurst {

    agent SecurityDesk

    every 1m {

        if AUTH_FAILURES_5M >= 50 {

            pause()

        }

    }

}
